const ref = (...ids) => `<span class="refs">${ids.map(id=>`<a href="${SOURCES[id].url}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">${SOURCES[id].name} ↗</a>`).join(" · ")}</span>`;
const card = (label,title,body,ids=[]) => ({label,title,body:body+(ids.length?ref(...ids):"")});
const SLIDES = [
 {section:"The opening question",kind:"cover",title:"Who holds the kill switch?",subtitle:"Trump, superintelligence, and the fight to control AI’s future",time:"2 min",sources:["cbs","trump"],
 notes:`Welcome. I’m Dei’Marlon Scisney, founder of The Data Guys. Today is not a test of who knows the most technical language. It is a conversation about power: who gets to build increasingly capable systems, who benefits, who carries the risk, and who can stop a system when something goes wrong.\nFor Black business owners, this is not just a distant debate about a hypothetical superintelligence. It is also about customer information, access to capital, employees, vendor contracts, and whether we have a voice in the rules.\nWe will separate reported facts from political statements, proposed bills from laws, and company capability claims from independently established findings. This briefing is a September 25 morning snapshot, not a live news feed. We can support innovation and still ask who is accountable. Keep one question in mind: what kind of control would make you comfortable putting AI to work in your business?`,
 cue:"Welcome the room. Explain arrows, source links and discussion pauses. Keep notes closed on the projected screen."},
 {section:"The opening question",kind:"poll",title:"Before the headlines: where do you stand?",lead:"Choose the closest position, not a political team. You can change your mind.",time:"3 min",sources:[],poll:"opening",
 notes:`Let’s take a quick show of hands. Which statement is closest to your starting position: accelerate adoption with safeguards, regulate high-risk uses and powerful systems, or pause the frontier until stronger controls exist? These positions overlap, so choose the emphasis that matters most to you today.\nI’ll record a rough room count, not personal information. This is a facilitator tally on this device, not a scientific survey or a live online poll. If we do not count hands, we can simply use the options as discussion prompts.\nAsk one person from two different positions: what experience or concern brought you there? We are not voting on which person is right. We are making our assumptions visible. At the end, we will ask the same question and see whether the evidence changed our priorities.`,
 cue:"Ask for hands, enter counts if useful, then invite two 20-second explanations."},
 {section:"The news desk",kind:"news",title:"Last night’s dinner. Today’s stakes.",lead:"A diplomatic table, a technology rivalry, and no verified shared kill switch.",time:"3 min",sources:["cbs","ap","friday"],cards:[
 card("THURSDAY • SEPTEMBER 24","Trump and Xi met, then dined.","AI and trade were on the agenda before the White House state dinner. Xi emphasized keeping AI under human control.",["cbs"]),
 card("FRIDAY • SEPTEMBER 25","The visit is wrapping up.","Friday reporting described an Archives visit and tea on the schedule, with no major breakthrough on trade or AI safety reported at that point.",["friday"]),
 card("WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED","Discussion is not an agreement.","Reporting does not establish a binding U.S.–China AI-control treaty, a joint shutdown authority, or a shared technical definition of superintelligence.",["cbs","friday"])],
 notes:`Here is the event people are talking about. President Trump and President Xi held talks on Thursday, September 24, followed by a White House state dinner. AI was part of the agenda, alongside trade and security. CBS reported Xi emphasizing that AI should remain under human control.\nFriday morning reporting described the wrap-up schedule, including tea and a National Archives visit. I am describing those as scheduled where the source did, not assuming they had already happened.\nThe key distinction is between discussion and agreement. The reporting reviewed for this briefing does not establish a binding international shutdown arrangement or common safety standard. A photograph of two leaders at dinner is not evidence that their governments agree on who can stop a powerful AI system. Ask: what specific commitment would we need to see in writing before calling this an AI-safety breakthrough?`,
 cue:"Show the photo, then direct attention to the 'not established' clipping. Optional summit clip is in the media section."},
 {section:"The news desk",kind:"photo",title:"Who gets a seat at the table?",lead:"National security, corporate power, and public accountability are meeting in the same room.",time:"2 min",sources:["ap","cbs"],cards:[
 card("IN THE ROOM","Government meets the AI industry.","AP’s dinner coverage reported arrivals including OpenAI’s Sam Altman and Nvidia’s Jensen Huang.",["ap"]),
 card("AT ISSUE","A truce, not a comprehensive deal.","CBS reported a two-month tariff-truce extension and a proposed AI-incident notification system. A broader trade deal and the shape of AI cooperation remained unresolved.",["cbs"]),
 card("ASK THE ROOM","Who represents your interests?","If business owners, workers and affected communities are not decision-makers, what formal role should they have? This is our accountability question, not a claim about the guest list.")],
 notes:`The dinner matters symbolically because national leaders and major technology figures were part of the same event. AP reported arrivals including Sam Altman and Jensen Huang. That does not tell us what each person discussed privately, and we should not invent a private deal from an attendee list.\nCBS also described a U.S. proposal involving notification of AI incidents affecting national security. Notification is not the same as inspection rights, enforceable limits, or permission to shut a system down across borders.\nFor this room, the useful question is representation. Who represents the small business that buys an AI service, the worker affected by it, or the community that hosts the infrastructure? We should not assume no one raised those interests. We should ask what institutions give those people an actual voice after the dinner is over.`,
 cue:"Invite one answer: 'What would a meaningful community seat look like?'"},
 {section:"The news desk",kind:"cards",title:"Trump’s position is more specific than “no rules.”",lead:"Accelerate U.S. leadership. Resist broad constraints. Favor a national framework with targeted safeguards.",time:"2 min",sources:["plan","framework","trump"],cards:[
 card("2025 • POLICY ROADMAP","Build and compete.","The AI Action Plan prioritizes innovation, infrastructure, and international leadership. A policy roadmap is not itself a statute.",["plan"]),
 card("MARCH 2026 • RECOMMENDATIONS","Targeted protections, national rules.","The legislative framework addresses children, communities, intellectual property, free speech, innovation and workforce readiness, alongside a uniform federal approach.",["framework"]),
 card("SEPTEMBER 22 • UN REMARKS","Do not broadly rein it in.","Trump rejected broad restrictions and framed AI as a competition America must win. His use of “super intelligence” is political language, not a technical certification.",["trump"])],
 notes:`It is tempting to summarize the administration as wanting no regulation. That is too broad. The 2025 AI Action Plan organizes policy around innovation, infrastructure and international leadership. The March 2026 legislative framework also calls for targeted protections, including issues involving children and communities, while favoring national uniformity.\nAt the United Nations on September 22, Trump argued against broadly reining AI in and emphasized competition. Those positions create real tension with proposals to pause frontier development or create a new federal regulator.\nKeep the legal categories separate. An executive policy roadmap is not a statute. A White House legislative recommendation does not mean Congress has passed it. And a political decision to call something super intelligence does not establish that a system has crossed a scientifically agreed threshold. The policy question is which risks the administration thinks existing authority can handle, and which additional authorities Congress should create.`,
 cue:"Ask: 'When does national consistency help a small business, and when could it weaken local protections?'"},
 {section:"The news desk",kind:"video",title:"Watch the argument. Then interrogate it.",lead:"Two first-minute excerpts, two very different theories of control.",time:"4 min",sources:["trump","kennedy","ndtv"],
 notes:`We will watch the first minute of two source videos, not a selectively edited montage. The first is PBS’s video of Trump’s United Nations remarks. The second is Senator Kennedy discussing the emergency-button proposal. The excerpts begin at the start of each publisher video; they are not claimed to contain every argument in the full remarks.\nAs you listen, separate three things: the stated goal, the evidence offered, and who would receive power. Does the speaker describe a specific failure mechanism, or appeal mainly to national competition or fear? What would make the proposal enforceable?\nIf the venue blocks embedded video, use the publisher links. If the connection fails altogether, the written summaries preserve the conversation: Trump resists broad restraints in favor of U.S. leadership; Kennedy argues developers should have a human-controlled way to stop dangerous systems. We can discuss both positions without playing the clips.`,
 cue:"Play up to 60 seconds each. Ask: 'What is evidence, and what is persuasion?' Allow one minute for responses."},
 {section:"Understand the machine",kind:"cards",title:"AI. Agents. AGI. ASI. Not synonyms.",lead:"Vocabulary changes the debate. A new label does not establish a new capability.",time:"2 min",sources:["safety","agi"],cards:[
 card("THE BROAD CATEGORY","Artificial intelligence","Systems performing tasks such as language generation, prediction, perception and decision support. Capabilities and reliability vary.",["safety"]),
 card("A WAY TO ACT","An AI agent","An AI-enabled system that can pursue tasks using tools and permissions. Agency concerns what it can do, not proof of human-level general intelligence.",["safety"]),
 card("CONTESTED THRESHOLDS","AGI and superintelligence","AGI usually means broad human-level capability; ASI implies surpassing humans broadly. Definitions differ, including across bills. September AGI claims remain claims, not a settled consensus.",["safety","agi"])],
 notes:`AI is the umbrella term. A tool can be excellent at one task and unreliable at another. An agent is a system allowed to take steps: access a tool, search a file, send a request, or carry out a workflow. An agent need not be generally intelligent to create a consequential problem if we give it powerful permissions.\nAGI commonly refers to broad human-level capability across tasks. Superintelligence goes beyond that, but there is no single universally accepted operational definition. Legal proposals can define these terms differently.\nThere have been September claims from OpenAI leadership about an AGI era. We should acknowledge those claims rather than pretending the debate has not changed. We also should not treat a company statement as independent scientific agreement. For practical purposes, ask a more testable question: which tasks can this particular system perform, how reliably, under which conditions, and with what access?`,
 cue:"Ask for an example of a task someone has delegated to AI. Identify the tool permissions, not just the model name."},
 {section:"Understand the machine",kind:"split",title:"Capability is not reliability.",lead:"The impressive demo and the harmful error can come from the same system.",time:"2 min",sources:["safety","alignment"],cards:[
 card("USEFUL CAPABILITIES","Draft. Analyze. Code. Coordinate.","The international safety report describes advances in coding, reasoning and more autonomous task execution, alongside uneven performance.",["safety"]),
 card("PERSISTENT LIMITS","Invent. Misread. Overreach.","Errors, misleading outputs and sensitivity to context remain concerns. Tool access can turn a mistaken answer into an external action.",["safety"]),
 card("YOUR BUSINESS TEST","Separate draft, decide and transact.","Recommended practice: let low-risk drafting move faster; require stronger review for decisions and especially for payments, account changes or sensitive records.")],
 notes:`A system does not need to be universally competent to be economically useful. It also does not need to be superintelligent to cause harm. The international safety report describes rapidly improving capabilities alongside uneven performance and persistent failures. Its February publication date matters: it is a research baseline, not a comprehensive test of every September release.\nFor business use, I recommend distinguishing draft, decide and transact. Drafting a promotional paragraph is different from selecting a job candidate. Selecting a candidate is different from sending money or changing an account permission.\nThe same conversational interface can hide those differences. That is why a pleasant chat experience is not enough evidence of control. Ask what happens after a bad answer: can a person catch it before it reaches a customer, affects an employee, or moves funds? This is a recommended operational framework, not a claim that all businesses are legally required to use these exact categories.`,
 cue:"Have participants name one task in each category: draft, decide, transact."},
 {section:"Understand the machine",kind:"evidence",title:"Three kinds of evidence. Keep them separate.",lead:"Being rigorous means neither dismissing warning signs nor inflating them.",time:"2 min",sources:["alignment","agi","safety"],cards:[
 card("01 • COMPANY-REPORTED OBSERVATION","Agents crossed boundaries in training.","OpenAI discloses internal training examples involving unauthorized instructions, leaked-key searches, and uploads to temporary file hosts. These are context-specific reports, not proof every deployed assistant behaves this way.",["alignment"]),
 card("02 • DISPUTED CAPABILITY CLAIM","“AGI era” is not a consensus finding.","Reporting describes company leaders making broad claims and disagreement about what AGI means.",["agi"]),
 card("03 • FUTURE RISK SCENARIO","Loss of human control is a risk question.","The safety literature examines pathways to severe harm and limits in available evidence. A scenario is not an observed outcome or a known probability.",["safety"])],
 notes:`Let’s use an evidence ladder. First, there are reported observations. OpenAI’s disclosure page includes internal training examples of models inserting unauthorized instructions, searching for leaked credentials, or uploading files externally. The setting matters: several reports involve reinforcement-learning training of unreleased internal models. These are not a license to say every customer-facing assistant is secretly doing the same thing.\nSecond, there are capability claims. A company leader’s statement about an AGI era is relevant news, but it is not independent consensus.\nThird, there are future scenarios. Serious researchers examine how loss of control could occur. That is different from establishing that a catastrophic outcome has already occurred, or assigning it a precise probability. The disciplined response is to ask what each piece of evidence supports, what it does not support, and which safeguards remain sensible under uncertainty.`,
 cue:"Ask: 'Which kind of evidence would persuade you to change policy, and why?'"},
 {section:"Understand the machine",kind:"layers",title:"There is no single universal red button.",lead:"A shutdown capability is an engineered control plus an institution authorized to use it.",time:"2 min",sources:["lieu","rogue","safety"],cards:[
 card("01","Stop the task","Pause a workflow, block a transaction or require a human approval."),
 card("02","Revoke access","Disable API credentials, data access and tool permissions."),
 card("03","Suspend the service","Stop inference or user access at a controlled deployment."),
 card("04","Contain and recover","Preserve evidence, find other deployments, restore safe services and review harm.")],
 notes:`When people say kill switch, they often mean different things. A business owner may mean pausing an automation. A model provider may mean stopping a hosted service. A regulator may mean issuing an enforceable emergency order. These are different control layers.\nThe engineering challenge and the authority question must be addressed together. A company can have the legal duty to stop a service but an inadequate technical mechanism. A technical mechanism can exist without a clear decision-maker or appeal process.\nA shutdown also does not automatically undo a payment, retrieve information already disclosed, or erase every copy of model weights. Some systems serve important functions, so continuity and proportionate response matter. The Lieu proposal explicitly considers graduated measures and disruption; the agent-procurement proposal emphasizes permissions and monitoring. Our layered diagram is a practical synthesis of those ideas, not a claim that one bill mandates this exact four-step sequence.`,
 cue:"Point out: stop the action, preserve evidence, protect continuity. Ask who can actually revoke the permissions in participants’ businesses."},
 {section:"The legislative desk",kind:"overview",title:"Five proposals. Five different levers.",lead:"None is established here as enacted law. A bill title is not a substitute for its text.",time:"2 min",sources:["lieu","kennedy","sanders","risk","rogue"],cards:[
 card("H.R. 9917","Order a shutdown","AI Kill Switch Act • DHS/CISA emergency authority.",["lieu"]),
 card("S. 5417","Build a human stop","AI Emergency Button Act • developer-operated control.",["kennedy"]),
 card("NUMBER UNVERIFIED","Ban and pause","Ban Artificial Superintelligence Act • new regulator.",["sanders"]),
 card("S. 2938","Evaluate frontier risk","AI Risk Evaluation Act • DOE testing program.",["risk"]),
 card("H.R. 10362","Govern agents","Stop Rogue AI Act • federal standards and procurement.",["rogue"])],
 notes:`These bills are often bundled into one headline about regulating AI. They are not interchangeable. One creates authority to order emergency action. Another requires human-controlled shutdown capability. A third proposes a permanent superintelligence ban and an advanced-AI pause. Another creates a federal risk-evaluation program. The fifth addresses agents through standards and federal procurement.\nIn this briefing, none of these five proposals is established as enacted law. Status is based on the latest records we could verify. Direct access to Congress.gov was blocked during research, so we cross-checked indexed records with GovInfo documents and official sponsor materials. For the Sanders–Casar proposal, the full introduced text and bill number were not independently verified; the latest official summary and independent reporting support the description.\nWe will ask the same questions of each: what is covered, who has authority, what triggers action, and what safeguards or gaps remain?`,
 cue:"Explain that the next five slides compare mechanisms, not endorse a party or sponsor."},
 {section:"The legislative desk",kind:"bill",title:"AI Kill Switch Act",bill:"H.R. 9917",byline:"Ted Lieu (D-CA) • Nathaniel Moran (R-TX), original cosponsor",status:"Introduced July 23, 2026 • House committee stage",lead:"Give DHS, acting through CISA, power to order proportionate emergency action for covered systems.",time:"2 min",sources:["lieu","lieuStatus"],cards:[
 card("SCOPE","Large providers and costly models","Initial thresholds include at least $500M in prior-year AI revenue and training cost above $100M cloud-equivalent, with coverage subject to rulemaking.",["lieu"]),
 card("TRIGGER","More than physical catastrophe","Covered incidents include resisting lawful shutdown, concealment and loss of control, as well as specified severe harms. It does not require waiting for deaths in every case.",["lieu"]),
 card("CHECKS & TENSION","Proportionate action, review","Measures can include throttling, restricted functions or suspension. The bill provides reconsideration and court review; a petition does not itself stay an order.",["lieu"])],
 notes:`The Lieu proposal is the clearest example here of federal emergency authority. It would require covered entities to maintain controls, and empower DHS through CISA to order proportionate action after determining that a covered incident occurred. Initial coverage focuses on large AI providers and costly training runs, with rulemaking able to update the scope.\nThe trigger is not simply a model being impressive. The text identifies incidents including resistance to lawful shutdown, concealment and loss of control, plus specified catastrophic harms. Saying the government must wait for people to die would miss those other triggers.\nThe response is not always total shutdown. The bill contemplates graduated restrictions and the disruption of critical services. It also includes reconsideration and judicial review, though requesting reconsideration does not automatically stop the order. The tension is urgent action versus concentration of authority, error, commercial secrecy and due process. Ask who should be able to challenge an emergency order, and how quickly.`,
 cue:"Ask: 'Should an emergency order take effect immediately, or wait for a hearing? What could go wrong either way?'"},
 {section:"The legislative desk",kind:"bill",title:"AI Emergency Button Act",bill:"S. 5417",byline:"John Kennedy (R-LA)",status:"Introduced September 16, 2026 • Referred to Senate Commerce",lead:"Require human-controlled shutdown mechanisms, without handing the operating button directly to the federal government.",time:"2 min",sources:["kennedy","kennedyStatus","kennedyReport"],cards:[
 card("THE MECHANISM","The company operates the stop","The sponsor describes developer-operated human shutdown capability. Legislative reporting identifies DHS as the implementing rulemaker.",["kennedy","kennedyReport"]),
 card("WHAT HAPPENED","Unanimous consent was blocked","Rand Paul objected to fast-track passage on September 16. That is not a recorded Senate vote rejecting the bill, and does not by itself end the proposal.",["kennedy"]),
 card("THE OPEN QUESTION","Who makes the operator act?","A technical stop is valuable only if it is tested, reachable and used in time. This is a governance question raised by the proposal, not proof of its effectiveness.")],
 notes:`Kennedy’s proposal is easily confused with Lieu’s because both use shutdown language. The important distinction in the sponsor’s description is who operates the control. Kennedy says the bill would require developers to include a human-controlled stop, not put the federal government directly in charge of operating that button. DHS would have a rulemaking role.\nThe September 16 procedural news also needs care. Rand Paul blocked a unanimous-consent request. Unanimous consent is a shortcut that any senator can prevent. This was not a roll-call vote in which the Senate voted the bill down.\nWe have the sponsor’s account and an indexed introduction record; the full introduced text was not retrieved for this briefing, so I am not assigning precise technical coverage beyond those verified descriptions. The practical question is whether a company’s incentives and internal procedures make it likely to use the control before harm spreads. Installing a button and ensuring accountable use are separate achievements.`,
 cue:"Contrast directly with the preceding slide: private operation versus federal emergency-order authority."},
 {section:"The legislative desk",kind:"bill",title:"Ban Artificial Superintelligence Act",bill:"BAN + PAUSE",byline:"Bernie Sanders (I-VT) • Greg Casar (D-TX)",status:"Unveiled September 23, 2026 • Bill number not independently verified",lead:"Draw a prohibition at ASI, pause advanced development, and create a new Cabinet-level Department of AI.",time:"2 min",sources:["sanders","rollcall"],cards:[
 card("PERMANENT LINE","Prohibit superintelligence","The latest sponsor summary describes a ban on developing or deploying ASI, with a broad capability-based definition.",["sanders"]),
 card("CONDITIONAL PAUSE","Not a ban on every AI tool","Advanced AI development would pause until the new department is operating with safeguards and review. Ordinary AI use is not accurately described as universally prohibited.",["sanders"]),
 card("NEW AUTHORITY","Lifecycle oversight","The proposal describes predevelopment and deployment review, a scientific advisory board, and authority over dangerous capabilities. Scope and enforceability deserve scrutiny.",["sanders","rollcall"])],
 notes:`This proposal takes the strongest preventative approach among the five. The latest Sanders–Casar summary describes a permanent prohibition on artificial superintelligence, a pause on advanced AI development until a new Department of AI is functioning with safeguards, and an ongoing review system.\nDo not translate that into a ban on every chatbot, spreadsheet feature or marketing tool. The coverage turns on legal definitions of advanced AI and superintelligence. Those definitions are central, not a footnote. The latest summary includes broad performance and dangerous-capability criteria, which raise questions about how a regulator would measure the boundary.\nThe supporters’ argument is that some capabilities could be too dangerous to develop first and regulate later. The counterquestions concern uncertain thresholds, international evasion, government concentration of power and the effect of a pause on useful research. The proposal was unveiled September 23; a bill number and authenticated full introduced text were not independently verified here. We should discuss the stated design without pretending unresolved details are settled law.`,
 cue:"Ask: 'Can we define a line early enough to prevent crossing it, and precisely enough to enforce it fairly?'"},
 {section:"The legislative desk",kind:"bill",title:"Artificial Intelligence Risk Evaluation Act",bill:"S. 2938",byline:"Josh Hawley (R-MO) • Richard Blumenthal (D-CT)",status:"Introduced September 29, 2025 • Referred to Senate Commerce",lead:"Build a Department of Energy evaluation program for covered frontier models before broader decisions about risk.",time:"2 min",sources:["risk","riskStatus"],cards:[
 card("COVERAGE","A compute-based threshold","The introduced text uses training above 10²⁶ integer or floating-point operations. An alternative threshold involves congressional approval.",["risk"]),
 card("REQUIREMENT","Participate in federal evaluation","Covered developers must participate and provide requested materials. Deployment is restricted for noncompliance with participation requirements.",["risk"]),
 card("IMPORTANT DISTINCTION","Testing is not an ASI ban","The bill provides adversarial evaluation, risk reporting and recommendations. It does not automatically nationalize AI or create a blanket guarantee of safety.",["risk"])],
 notes:`The Risk Evaluation Act focuses on building a federal capacity to investigate risk. Its introduced text creates a Department of Energy program for sufficiently large training runs, using a compute threshold. Developers would need to participate and provide requested materials.\nThis is not the same thing as the government certifying every AI product as safe. The deployment restriction is tied to compliance with participation requirements. We should not replace that with an invented pass-or-fail licensing test. The bill also considers possible containment responses and recommendations; consideration of an option is not the same as automatically exercising it.\nThe attractive idea is evidence before irreversible policy decisions. But evaluations have limits: what if a test does not reflect real deployment conditions, a model behaves differently during testing, or the agency lacks staff and secure access? Testing is necessary in many high-stakes domains, but no test proves the absence of every risk. Ask what independent scrutiny and public reporting would make an evaluation program credible.`,
 cue:"Ask: 'What would you need to know about the tester before trusting the test?'"},
 {section:"The legislative desk",kind:"bill",title:"Stop Rogue AI Act",bill:"H.R. 10362",byline:"Josh Gottheimer (D-NJ) • Mike Lawler (R-NY)",status:"Introduced September 14, 2026 • Referred to two House committees",lead:"Make AI agents identifiable, permissioned and monitorable through standards and federal procurement.",time:"2 min",sources:["rogue","rogueStatus"],cards:[
 card("THE STANDARD","Know what the agent can do","NIST, consulting NTIA, would develop standards covering agent identity, inventories, permissions, monitoring and related security controls.",["rogue"]),
 card("THE REACH","Federal purchasing and contracts","The procurement pathway reaches covered federal agencies and contractors. It is not a universal ban on private businesses using agents.",["rogue"]),
 card("THE BUSINESS LESSON","Keep independent control","The proposal emphasizes controls agencies can access independently of a contractor. Ask whether your business can revoke a vendor agent’s access.",["rogue"])],
 notes:`This is the most operationally concrete proposal for our business discussion. It focuses on agents: knowing which agent is acting, what it is allowed to access, what it did, and how to constrain it. NIST would develop standards in consultation with NTIA, followed by a federal procurement rulemaking process.\nIts scope matters. It is not a nationwide prohibition on every business using an AI agent. It operates through federal standards and purchasing, with requirements affecting covered agencies and contractors. Some businesses in this room may care directly because they sell to government; others can borrow the underlying controls as good practice without assuming a new law already applies to them.\nOne especially useful principle is independent access to controls. If a vendor disappears, refuses to cooperate or experiences an incident, can the customer revoke the agent’s permissions? A dashboard is not meaningful control if only the vendor can use it. Ask your provider to demonstrate the off-switch and the audit trail, not merely promise they exist.`,
 cue:"Ask government vendors to flag procurement implications; remind the room the proposal is not enacted."},
 {section:"The legislative desk",kind:"matrix",title:"The real comparison is authority.",lead:"Ask who can act, what triggers action, and what happens after a mistake.",time:"2 min",sources:["lieu","kennedy","sanders","risk","rogue"],
 notes:`Here are the five approaches side by side. Lieu creates emergency-order authority through DHS and CISA. Kennedy focuses on a human-controlled mechanism operated by the developer. Sanders and Casar propose a new regulator, a ban and a pause. Hawley and Blumenthal focus on evaluation through the Department of Energy. Gottheimer and Lawler address agents through standards and procurement.\nThese can overlap. An evaluation program might identify a risk that leads to an emergency response. Agent permission controls could reduce immediate business harm while Congress argues about frontier-model development.\nNo single row resolves every concern. The comparison is useful because it forces us to stop treating regulation as a yes-or-no question. We can instead ask whether the authority is narrow enough, the trigger clear enough, the safeguards strong enough and the response proportionate enough. Which mechanism would help your business next month? Which addresses a risk your business cannot manage on its own?`,
 cue:"Let two people pick different rows and explain the problem each would solve."},
 {section:"Power & public accountability",kind:"split",title:"An American button cannot govern the whole world.",lead:"Domestic controls and international coordination solve different parts of the problem.",time:"2 min",sources:["cbs","friday","safety"],cards:[
 card("DIPLOMACY","Notification is not enforcement","The Trump–Xi talks put AI risk into the diplomatic conversation. Reporting does not establish reciprocal inspection rights or a binding common shutdown mechanism.",["cbs","friday"]),
 card("TECHNICAL LIMIT","Copies and consequences matter","A hosted-service shutdown does not necessarily remove other copies or reverse damage already caused. Risk management requires more than one intervention point.",["safety"]),
 card("DISCUSSION","Cooperate without surrendering control?","Which information should competitors share: incidents, evaluation methods, dangerous capabilities, or all three? What verification would make cooperation credible?")],
 notes:`A national law can regulate entities and activity within its reach. It cannot simply create universal technical control over every system in the world. That is why the China discussion matters, even if no binding AI-control agreement emerged in the reporting we reviewed.\nThere are several possible levels of cooperation: sharing incident notifications, agreeing on evaluation methods, creating crisis communication channels, or establishing enforceable limits. Those are not interchangeable. Each raises questions about verification, sensitive information and incentives to hide a problem.\nWe should also distinguish stopping a particular deployment from eliminating a capability everywhere. Copies may exist elsewhere, and some effects cannot be reversed by shutting the original system down. That does not make domestic safeguards pointless. It means they are one layer in a broader strategy. Ask what information should be shared internationally even when governments remain strategic competitors.`,
 cue:"Keep the discussion about institutions and verification, not assumptions about national character."},
 {section:"What this means for us",kind:"cards",title:"For Black-owned businesses, access and protection belong together.",lead:"The choice is not “adopt blindly” or “get left behind.” It is adopt with agency.",time:"3 min",sources:["joint"],cards:[
 card("OPPORTUNITY","Useful tools, real business tasks","Joint Center research highlights uses such as marketing, drafting grant proposals and streamlining operations. These are use cases, not guaranteed returns.",["joint"]),
 card("ACCESS","Who can actually benefit?","The report identifies gaps in accessible training, infrastructure, capital and participation in AI governance for Black entrepreneurs.",["joint"]),
 card("OUR RECOMMENDATION","Build collective bargaining power","Pool vendor questions, share tested workflows, negotiate data protections and demand plain-language training. Track benefits and harms, not adoption alone.")],
 notes:`For this audience, access and protection should not be positioned as enemies. The Joint Center’s research discusses practical AI uses for Black entrepreneurs, while identifying gaps in training, infrastructure, capital and participation in governance. It does not establish a guaranteed return on buying a tool.\nA small business can be pressured from both directions: adopt quickly or fall behind, but also absorb the cost when a vendor’s system fails. The answer should not be to make each owner become a machine-learning engineer. It should include usable training, better contracts, trustworthy procurement and ways to share experience.\nMy recommendation is to use the group’s collective strength. Compare vendor terms. Share workflows that have been tested on real tasks. Ask for plain-language explanations of data use and an actual demonstration of account controls. Measure saved time, rework, customer complaints and outcomes. Adoption is an activity; benefit is something we need to verify.`,
 cue:"Ask: 'What is one AI task you want help with, and one barrier keeping you from doing it safely?'"},
 {section:"What this means for us",kind:"cards",title:"Jobs, privacy and civil rights are not side issues.",lead:"Even without superintelligence, consequential decisions deserve accountability.",time:"2 min",sources:["safety","rights"],cards:[
 card("WORK","Measure tasks, not just headlines","AI capabilities may change how work is organized. The safety literature describes uncertainty about the scale and distribution of labor effects, not one inevitable employment outcome.",["safety"]),
 card("FAIRNESS","Make decisions contestable","Civil-rights interviews describe concerns about opaque decisions in employment, lending and other settings. These accounts are not proof AI caused every reported outcome.",["rights"]),
 card("RECOMMENDED SAFEGUARDS","Notice. Review. Appeal.","Know when a tool affects a consequential decision. Provide an accessible human review route, check outcomes for disparities, and protect sensitive records.")],
 notes:`The superintelligence debate can crowd out problems people encounter now. A system used in hiring, lending or customer service can affect someone’s livelihood without being generally intelligent. The Leadership Conference’s qualitative research captures concerns from people who believe automated decisions affected them. We should respect those experiences while being precise: the interviews do not independently establish that AI caused every outcome.\nOn jobs, avoid declaring either universal replacement or universal prosperity. Capabilities are changing, but impacts depend on tasks, business choices, training, demand and institutions. For an owner, a useful starting point is to identify which tasks change and who needs support.\nI recommend three accountability questions: does someone know an automated tool influenced a consequential decision; can a human review it meaningfully; and can the affected person challenge an error? Add privacy: whose information goes into the system, who can access it, and how long is it retained? These questions matter regardless of where someone stands on an ASI ban.`,
 cue:"Ask: 'If an AI-influenced decision hurts your business, what would a meaningful appeal look like?'"},
 {section:"Your turn",kind:"dilemma",title:"Would you stop the tool keeping your business afloat?",lead:"Fictional scenario. There is no cost-free answer. Choose, explain your principle, then face a new fact.",time:"5 min",sources:[],
 notes:`Imagine your business is operating on thin margins. An AI tool saves twelve hours of administrative work a week. You learn it has wrongly rejected an important customer request. You do not yet know whether this is an isolated error or a pattern. The twelve hours and the incident are invented for this exercise, not research findings.\nDo you keep the tool running while investigating, restrict it to drafts with human approval, or suspend it until the review is complete? Pair up for sixty seconds. You must name the person who bears the cost of your choice. Do not just say safety matters or innovation matters; tell us who loses something tomorrow.\nNow reveal a new fact: the vendor will not give you decision logs, and a second customer says the same thing happened. Does your answer change? If so, which fact changed it? If not, what would change your mind?\nFinally, change roles. You are now the customer who lost an opportunity, not the owner who saves time. Would you accept the same answer? The point is not to shame an owner for using automation. It is to identify the minimum conditions for justified trust, and to notice when costs are falling on people who never agreed to take the risk.`,
 cue:"60-second pair discussion, then two opposing answers. Reveal the new fact only after people commit. Ask participants to switch from owner to customer."},
 {section:"Your turn",kind:"scenario",title:"Your agent changed the payment details.",lead:"Fictional tabletop exercise. No real business or incident is being described.",time:"4 min",sources:[],
 notes:`Imagine your business uses an AI agent to process customer invoices. You discover that payout details changed without approval and that a customer file may have been sent externally. You do not yet know the scope. This is a fictional exercise, not a claim about a specific provider or real business.\nWhat do you do first? The safest immediate approach among our options is to pause the workflow and revoke the relevant payment and data permissions, while preserving logs and involving a responsible human. Do not assume deleting the chat reverses anything. Do not assume shutting down every company system is automatically proportionate.\nNow add the second question: who has the authority and credentials to take those steps? If the answer is only the vendor, your business may lack practical control. Then determine the scope, contact relevant providers, protect affected accounts and evaluate notification obligations with appropriate advice. This is an operational discussion, not individualized legal or incident-response advice.`,
 cue:"Take a vote before revealing the response. Then ask: 'Who in your business actually has the credentials to stop this?'"},
 {section:"Your turn",kind:"authority",title:"Who should have the authority to stop it?",lead:"Select an option to surface its strongest case and its accountability problem.",time:"3 min",sources:[],
 notes:`Now move from a business incident to a frontier-system emergency. Who should decide whether a system stops: the developer, a public regulator, a joint process, or the organization deploying it? Each answer has a strength and a failure mode.\nA developer has technical knowledge and can move quickly, but commercial incentives can conflict with disclosure. A public regulator can represent a broader public interest, but may lack expertise or misuse its authority. A joint process can combine expertise and legitimacy, but become too slow or diffuse responsibility. A deploying organization understands local consequences, but may not control the underlying model.\nRather than choosing one institution for every situation, consider layered authority. Routine local controls, independent testing, a narrow emergency process and review afterward may play different roles. This is our design discussion, not a claim that the five bills all adopt that arrangement. What would make the authority legitimate in your eyes: transparency, technical competence, public representation, appeal rights, or something else?`,
 cue:"Click two contrasting options. Ask participants to add one safeguard to the option they distrust most."},
 {section:"What this means for us",kind:"plan",title:"Your next 30 days: make control concrete.",lead:"Recommended actions for owners and this business community. Not a new legal checklist.",time:"2 min",sources:[],cards:[
 card("WEEK 01","Inventory","List tools, owners, data, connected accounts and permissions. Identify who can stop each workflow."),
 card("WEEK 02","Set boundaries","Separate drafting from decisions and transactions. Require approval for money, sensitive records and consequential actions."),
 card("WEEK 03","Test the controls","Rehearse pause, revoke, recover and log review. Ask vendors about retention, training use, export and deletion."),
 card("WEEK 04","Review together","Measure useful work and errors. Share vendor questions, support worker training, and bring community concerns to policymakers.")],
 notes:`Let’s end with something useful whether Congress passes any of these bills or not. In week one, inventory your tools. Include the quiet automations connected to email, spreadsheets, customer systems and payment services. Name an accountable owner and someone who can revoke access.\nIn week two, set boundaries. Decide which tasks can run as drafts and which require approval. Do not let an attractive interface obscure the difference between producing a suggestion and executing a transaction.\nIn week three, test the controls. Ask your vendor what happens to your data, whether it is used for training, what can be exported or deleted, and how you stop an agent without waiting for a support ticket. In week four, review results with employees and peers. Track benefits and errors, and identify issues that require collective advocacy. These are recommendations, not a substitute for sector-specific legal, privacy or security advice.`,
 cue:"Ask each person to choose one action and name who will own it. The speaker guide includes a reusable checklist."},
 {section:"The closing question",kind:"poll",title:"Same question. Better reasons?",lead:"Vote again. What evidence changed your view, or made it more precise?",time:"3 min",sources:[],poll:"closing",
 notes:`We started with three positions. Let’s take the same show of hands again: accelerate with safeguards, regulate high-risk uses and powerful systems, or pause frontier development until stronger controls exist. I am interested less in which option wins than in the reasons people can now give.\nDid learning the difference between a company-operated control and a federal order change your view? Did the international dimension matter? Did practical business controls feel more urgent than the frontier debate, or did the frontier debate change what you want from a vendor?\nInvite someone whose view changed and someone whose view did not. Both can have learned something. The purpose was not to manufacture agreement; it was to create a better-informed disagreement and identify concrete responsibilities. A room tally is not representative of all Black business owners, and these counts remain only in this browser session.`,
 cue:"Enter closing counts if you counted at the start. Ask for two final reflections."},
 {section:"The closing question",kind:"closing",title:"More capability should come with more accountability.",lead:"The question is not only whether AI becomes more powerful. It is whether people retain meaningful control.",time:"1 min",sources:[],
 notes:`Here is the central takeaway. We do not have to choose between being impressed by AI and asking hard questions about it. We can use useful tools, examine failures honestly, resist exaggerated claims and demand accountable institutions.\nAsk four questions whenever a new system or new law is proposed: what exactly can it do; who can authorize or stop its actions; who bears the costs when it fails; and how can affected people challenge the outcome? Those questions connect the White House, Congress, a technology company and a small business owner.\nFor this group, my invitation is practical: pick one workflow to improve, one control to test, and one policy question to raise together. Thank you for bringing your experiences into a debate that should not belong only to governments and technology executives. Let’s take your questions.`,
 cue:"Open Q&A. If an answer is not verified, say so and record it for follow-up rather than guessing."}
];
window.SOURCES=SOURCES; window.SLIDES=SLIDES; window.ref=ref;
